  "live_records": {
    "q-140845": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140845",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all distinct eigenvalues\n\nII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u$ is parallel to $v.$\n\nIII. $A$ is diagonalizable.",
      "worked_solution": "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the spectral theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. Since the eigenvectors are nonzero, we can't have $u$ parallel to $v.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
