    "q-95411": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95411",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that $A$ is a real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ must have all real eigenvalues\n\nII. $A$ is diagonalizable\n\nIII. If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha $",
      "worked_solution": "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the spectral theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. Since the eigenvector are nonzero, we can't have $u=\\alpha v$ for some scalar $\\alpha .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
