    "q-95714": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95714",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+10I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-95714-a",
